universities have had their share of  headlines recently from allegations of  chinese influence renaming colleges  following donations no platforming of  speakers controversies over trans rights  and kathleen stock the professor at  sussex university  now oxford university finds itself at  the heart of the latest storm over  donations of more than 12 million pounds  from the mosley family trust which  inherited a fortune from the british  union of fascists leader sir oswald  mosley  and the storm is growing with fresh  revelations in the telegraph today that  both imperial college london and  university college london accepted  millions more from the trust  a senior oxford don now says the  university has lost its moral compass  and the government needs to act to  reform the whole sector he's professor  lawrence goldman emeritus professor at  saint peter's college he joins me  now  writing on the telegraph uh  professor goldman you say our  universities have lost their way and  need the charity commission and  government to intervene  decisively um  what exactly is the problem with max  mosley's money  well the problem is max mosley himself  and his relationship to the history of  british fascism um people might think of  him as a colorful character in british  public life he died in may of this year  somebody involved in motor racing but as  a young man having graduated from oxford  uh he joined his father's second attempt  at forming a fascist movement in the  late 1950s and early 1960s and he went  around terrorizing and intimidating  people on the streets of notting hill in  west london and on the streets of  dalston in east london  and he never apologized he was never  willing to atone for those crimes and  sins and in his autobiography he makes  it plain that he didn't think he he  actually um did do anything wrong that  was published in 2015 so it was very  surprising after further revelations  about the person that he was in the  press and in broadcasting media in 2018  that the university of oxford should  continue to take money from the mosley  family trust which he chaired up to his  death this year  yeah but um  your college saint peter's college uh  says  that whatever  the source of the money it will be  transformative for students  it is a lot of money and perhaps it will  benefit from some students one of the  other colleges says the mostly money uh  will benefit what they call diverse  students by which i think they mean  minority or poor students  yeah well i i understand that and that  argument is always used in circumstances  like this but don't you think actually  that that money should go to the  communities who were terrorized uh and  beaten up by mosley and his thugs twice  in the 20th century in the 1930s and the  early 1960s if the mosley family trusts  want to atone if they want to do good in  the world surely they should be building  civic centers in notting hill or old age  homes for elderly jews who were beaten  up in golda's green and northwest london  isn't that really the way the money  should be used oxford has lots of money  and can continue to get money from other  sources it does all the time  and i don't really buy the argument that  because you can do some good in oxford  you should just continue to hold on to  what is essentially tainted and dirty  money  but  it might be said the best repost to um  as it were fascist money let's call it  that is to use it to support the the  victims of fascism or the the people  that fascists would like to see expunged  from universities  yeah absolutely i completely agree i  have nothing against helping the  students of saint peter's i spent my  career helping the students of saint  peter's but they're not really the  people who should be the worthy  recipients of these funds what should  have happened was that long ago max  mosley should have apologized for his  actions he should have fessed up to what  he has done and then he should have used  that money very productively uh to  assist those who were as it were on the  receiving end the victims of that  fascist violence and then also if he  wished to he could have supported uh uh  colleges in oxford and the university uh  nothing against people apologizing and  atoning for their sins and then using  their money productively but without  that apology i don't really think this  money belongs in oxford oxford should  live by it its professions uh its values  it its academic integrity and it should  reject funds like this which are still  tainted  you accused oxford of losing its moral  compass and uh much as those of us who  are graduates of other universities  would like uh all wickedness to  to reside in oxford and indeed cambridge  it is true that other universities  london school of economics a university  college and indeed my own alma mater  imperial college have got mostly money  are you saying that all of them and the  sector as a whole is losing its moral  compass that's a big claim  well i suspect i am i wasn't aware of  the degree to which this money had been  spread across the system but you do have  to ask how it can be that so many of our  leading uh universities seem not to  recognize the significance of the name  of mosley when donations are put in in  front of them seem not to recognize that  there are many communities in britain  and around the world who will feel  insulted that this money is being used  by the universities without an apology  uh so i do think there is a broad  problem the university sector has to be  much more careful  so in short you're saying give it back  and if they don't give it back  would you um ask mr zahawi the education  secretary to step in and make sure that  it is handed back and the taint is  removed  yes i'm a historian and i know although  this may seem arcane to your viewers  that in the mid and late 19th century  oxford and cambridge had to be reformed  by the state there were a succession of  royal commissions uh the problems of  non-functioning universities as they  were then were reformed and they were  turned into great powerhouses of  research and teaching and development  that we've come to know and admire and i  do think there's a role for the state i  i believe in self-governance i believe  these universities should largely be  self-governing but if they can't govern  themselves effectively and according to  the moral principles that i think most  british people would expect of great  universities then there may be a role  for the state indeed  okay professor goldman thank you very  much indeed  thank you
 

